WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.932 --> 00:00:07.390
My name is Colin
Gillespie, and I'm

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:09.940
going to be talking about
comparing package versions.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:11.648
I worked for jumping
rivers, essentially,

00:00:11.648 --> 00:00:13.523
this slide can be summarized
as "we do everything

00:00:13.523 --> 00:00:14.390
with R and Python

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:15.482
if you need help with that".

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:16.940
If you've been
around the exhibit

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:22.900
stands, we also do a smashing
side project on coasters.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:28.270
And do you know how hard it is to get
500 coasters through US customs?

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.280
It's surprisingly
difficult, even when

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.420
you say you're going to
an RStudio conference.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:33.960
You just think that
should explain it all.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:34.900
So if you want to
have that story,

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.520
come and have a chat as well.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:40.300
So we all love R.
You start your project,

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:44.920
you pull in wonderful packages,
you do some amazing code,

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:46.860
and then you're about
to present your results and

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.900
you think "I could just
update my packages?"

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.010
What is the worst
that could happen?

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.190
Surely, well, not a
lot could have changed

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:57.700
where we've all been there.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:00.420
And you've gone from praising
the R gods to swearing at them

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.490
and cursing them and
hating everything about it.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.260
And essentially
what the question

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.170
is when a package is updated.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.020
How do you find
out what's changed?

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.180
So it should be
an easy question.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.010
How do you find out what's changed?

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:18.787
Well, if the gods are smiling
in your favor and the package

00:01:18.787 --> 00:01:19.870
author has been wonderful.

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:20.830
So here is lubridate.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:22.740
You can see you've
got a nice news file.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.840
My news files are not like this.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.370
I wish they were, but you
can sort of see it's nice.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:30.597
Links to issues, new
features, bug fixes.

00:01:30.597 --> 00:01:33.180
It's all very good, it's great,
you know, you clickable links,

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.483
it tells you exactly
what's going on.

00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:36.900
They may even be
a blogpost

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.730
by the authors detailing
exactly what's changed.

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:41.730
And you could walk
out and that's good.

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.980
So that's if you're lucky,
the birds are singing,

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:45.390
the sun shining.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.860
If you're unlucky.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.510
Well you may have to
go to the git repo.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.720
This is lubridate's git
repo. But just trying

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.220
to keep the consistent so.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:55.790
And that's not bad, right?

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:56.890
You know, in the grand
scheme of things,

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:58.570
going through someone's
git repo is definitely

00:01:58.570 --> 00:01:59.403
better than nothing.

00:01:59.403 --> 00:02:01.540
And it's OK.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.060
You may just be relying on
something like R Weekly,

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:07.070
for example, to help you
figure out what's going on.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:10.500
And that could be another
way of figuring out.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:14.580
And if you've done something
to upset essentially everyone,

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:17.503
then essentially download
the tar.qz file from CRAN

00:02:17.503 --> 00:02:19.920
and you try to sit there
and you try and work your way

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.240
through.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.430
And these people
here are essentially

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.040
showing fake consultant
happiness. So fake consultant

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.140
happiness and may mean
this when your client asks

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.450
you to do something you really
don't want to answer, you just

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.360
go, "Yes, that's fine.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:32.683
Of course I'll do that."

00:02:32.683 --> 00:02:34.600
So if you've ever gone
through the source code,

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.670
It's not a fun process because
you're trying to figure it out.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.320
And this is where I diffify came in.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:40.982
So this is diffify.com.

00:02:40.982 --> 00:02:42.440
And essentially
it's trying to make

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.550
that package difference easier.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.390
So you go to diffify.com,
you click on R,

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:52.490
and then you put in your package
that you want. This a team

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:53.010
effort.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:56.078
So it's really about 10
contributors and all of them

00:02:56.078 --> 00:02:58.370
done amazing job at coding
with the exception of me, who is

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:01.125
standing here presenting, has
done no coding whatsoever.

00:03:01.125 --> 00:03:03.500
So I'm loving the dream of
not doing any of the real work

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:04.620
but standing presenting it.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:05.690
So thank you to
all the other nine

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:06.992
people who have done the work.

00:03:06.992 --> 00:03:09.200
So as an example, I thought,
well, we go for lubridate,

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.380
so you type in lubridate.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.020
And that's a package.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.880
And then you get to choose which
version you want to compare.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:15.630
So you've got one.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.210
You're going from two.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:21.260
We've gone back to
roundabout, think it's 2017,

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:22.940
We're probably going to go...

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.610
We'll keep knocking off
a year until things start

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:28.130
breaking in the parsing
and then we'll probably not

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:29.880
go back any further. We reckon

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:33.770
around to 2014, but
just in 2017 is fine.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:38.410
So here we're going to
compare version 1.8 with 1.79.

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:40.360
The news is mostly
split up, so we can see

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:42.470
that it's been a few releases.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:45.882
1.792 wasn't actually on
CRAN, but that news file

00:03:45.882 --> 00:03:47.090
was kicking about somewhere.

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:50.290
So the news part.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:55.330
Highlights what's changed to
describe the news. More useful...

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.580
You can actually
start exploring what's

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:58.760
changed programmatically.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:01.070
So, sometimes it's not a news file.

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:03.820
Sometimes the news
file is incomplete.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:05.920
So here we can see
that rcpp has been

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:10.630
removed as an import from
lubridate over the last version.

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:12.340
We can look at
the namespace file

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.260
and again we get
the same idea here.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.930
So we've had four functions
added to the namespace file

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:18.649
in that version change that.

00:04:18.649 --> 00:04:20.800
We can have a look.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.050
We can look at the
functions and here we

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:25.660
can see where we've got four
functions added and one of them

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:29.477
has the arguments have changed
and then we can click on that.

00:04:29.477 --> 00:04:31.060
And we can see exactly
which arguments

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.480
have changed in this function.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.897
So here we can see that the
second positional argument has

00:04:34.897 --> 00:04:37.120
changed and that could cause
something seriously bad

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:38.330
to go with your code, right?

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:40.205
So if you're relying on
positional arguments,

00:04:40.205 --> 00:04:42.500
that could be a
bit of a problem.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.570
So the roadmap is the
next step is we're

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:46.770
going to have CICD for package steps.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:48.470
So essentially you're
working on GitHub

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:51.775
and you can automatically put
up your version 0.9000.

00:04:51.775 --> 00:04:53.150
And so you can
sort of see what's

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:54.380
happening to dev version.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.280
Python should have been
released last week.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:57.988
It's now going to be
released next month.

00:04:57.988 --> 00:04:58.927
That's life, isn't it?

00:04:58.927 --> 00:05:01.260
And essentially we've got a
whole bunch of other things.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:03.920
But if you've got ideas,
please come up and see or tweet

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.680
or email or anything like
that and we're really happy

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:08.850
for suggestions,
happy for questions.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:12.120
And feel free to go to diffify.com and get
a feel for what's going on.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.370
Thanks.

